<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                   http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

    <!--
        The empty <rollback/> blocks in this file are deliberate, not omissions.

        A changeset guarded by a MARK_RAN precondition can be recorded as ran without having applied its change, so
        its inverse must never run. With no rollback declared Liquibase auto-generates that inverse (dropColumn,
        dropTable, and so on) and a rollback would then destroy an object this changeset never created. An empty
        rollback leaves the object in place instead: inert, and re-applying the changeset is a no-op through the same
        precondition.
    -->

    <!--
      Give workspace_user an environment dimension. A NULL environment means "every environment" and is what every
      existing row has, so there is no data migration and no behaviour change on upgrade.

      Per (workspace_id, user_id) the membership is either implicit (a single NULL row) or explicit (one row per
      environment, an absent row meaning denial). That one-mode-at-a-time rule is a service-layer invariant; the
      schema below enforces only the weaker part of it, because a partial index cannot express "not both modes".

      Every changeset is precondition-guarded so that a database which already carries the change - a developer
      database patched by hand, or one where the superseded constraint was never created because
      202604061200060 hit its own MARK_RAN precondition - converges instead of failing at startup.
    -->

    <changeSet id="20260819120000" author="Ivica Cardic">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <columnExists tableName="workspace_user" columnName="environment"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <comment>Add the nullable environment ordinal to workspace_user. NULL means every environment.</comment>

        <addColumn tableName="workspace_user">
            <column name="environment" type="INT"/>
        </addColumn>
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260819120001" author="Ivica Cardic">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <indexExists tableName="workspace_user" indexName="uk_workspace_user_workspace_user"/>
        </preConditions>

        <comment>
            Drop the (workspace_id, user_id) unique constraint added by 202604061200060. It forbids the several rows
            per member that explicit mode requires. This is not a weakening: uk_workspace_user_implicit below
            re-imposes exactly the same rule over the NULL rows, which is every row that exists today.
        </comment>

        <dropUniqueConstraint tableName="workspace_user" constraintName="uk_workspace_user_workspace_user"/>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260819120002" author="Ivica Cardic">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <indexExists tableName="workspace_user" indexName="uk_workspace_user_implicit"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <comment>
            At most one implicit row per member. Written as raw SQL because Liquibase's createIndex has no WHERE
            clause, and a plain three-column unique constraint would not do: PostgreSQL treats NULLs as distinct and
            would admit unlimited NULL rows for one member.
        </comment>

        <sql>
            CREATE UNIQUE INDEX uk_workspace_user_implicit
                ON workspace_user (workspace_id, user_id) WHERE environment IS NULL;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS uk_workspace_user_implicit;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260819120003" author="Ivica Cardic">
        <preConditions onFail="MARK_RAN">
            <not>
                <indexExists tableName="workspace_user" indexName="uk_workspace_user_explicit"/>
            </not>
        </preConditions>

        <comment>At most one explicit row per member per environment.</comment>

        <sql>
            CREATE UNIQUE INDEX uk_workspace_user_explicit
                ON workspace_user (workspace_id, user_id, environment) WHERE environment IS NOT NULL;
        </sql>

        <rollback>
            <sql>DROP INDEX IF EXISTS uk_workspace_user_explicit;</sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>
</databaseChangeLog>
